Rem ==================================================================
Rem eng.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: engineering
Rem
Rem Continuous mathematics over Number: the trigonometry the runtime does
Rem not supply, hyperbolics, complex numbers, numeric calculus over
Rem quotations, root-finding and polynomial arithmetic, number theory,
Rem combinatorics and the discrete distributions, descriptive statistics,
Rem sequences, unit conversion and physical constants. Pure throughout.
Rem
Rem Conventions, all of them load-bearing:
Rem
Rem   * ANGLES ARE RADIANS AT EVERY BOUNDARY. There is no degree mode. A
Rem     mode is state, this machine has none, and a trig word whose answer
Rem     depends on a setting made three screens ago is the worst thing
Rem     about the calculators this surface was compiled from. EngRad,
Rem     EngDeg, EngGradOf and EngFromGrad convert at the edges and are the
Rem     only places 180 or 200 appears in an angle context.
Rem
Rem   * COEFFICIENTS ARE HIGHEST POWER FIRST in every polynomial word.
Rem     {1, 0, -4} is x^2 - 4. This is the one convention a caller will
Rem     get backwards, and backwards it evaluates without complaint to the
Rem     wrong number.
Rem
Rem   * NEVER COMPARE COMPLEX NUMBERS WITH =. Structural equality reports
Rem     EngComplex(0.1 + 0.2, 0) = EngComplex(0.3, 0) as False. EngCNear
Rem     is the only comparison offered, and it is the one to use on any
Rem     root, eigenvalue or transform output.
Rem
Rem   * SOLVERS REFUSE RATHER THAN MISLEAD. EngZeroOf checks its bracket
Rem     before descending; a solver that returns a plausible number for a
Rem     bracket with no root is the worst failure available here.
Rem
Rem   * THE SURFACE IS CLOSED. Every word is Eng*; no stats, math, str,
Rem     seq or dict name appears in a signature or return type. Builtins
Rem     are the language, not a machine, so Sin(x) at a call site is not a
Rem     leak. The wrappers over stats are mandatory rather than polite:
Rem     this machine includes stats, so a word named Mean would be a
Rem     duplicate declaration and the machine would not load.
Rem
Rem Three ceilings, each of which produces a plausible wrong answer rather
Rem than an error, so each is stated where it bites:
Rem
Rem   * 2^53 — the whole-number ceiling. EngFact(18) is the last exact
Rem     factorial; EngModPow, EngFactorize and EngComb are bounded by it
Rem     too, which is why EngComb multiplies and divides alternately
Rem     rather than dividing one factorial by two others.
Rem   * |x| > 710 — where EngSinh and EngCosh overflow, and where
Rem     EngGamma does; EngGammaLn keeps going and is the word to use.
Rem   * The numeric derivative's step. EngDeriv is accurate to about
Rem     eight digits and EngDeriv2 to about four. Neither is exact and
Rem     neither says so at the call site.
Rem
Rem Not here, deliberately, and each named so the gap reads as scoped
Rem rather than missing: number bases, bit operations, masks and truth
Rem tables are bool.shoddy; symbolic factoring, expansion and symbolic
Rem calculus are alg.shoddy; linear algebra over matrix.shoddy's Matrix
Rem is lin.shoddy; the hypothesis tests are stats.shoddy, which a caller
Rem who wants Anova or TTest2 includes and says so; random numbers are
Rem math.shoddy, and are left there because this machine is pure and
Rem wrapping Rnd would make that untrue. Polynomial arithmetic over
Rem coefficient lists is not symbolic algebra and ships here.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "stats.shoddy"
Include "math.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- choices ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem Shoddy has no enum type, so every choice is a zero-arg Def. These stay
Rem Defs rather than becoming top-level Lets: a Number compares by value
Rem and costs nothing to push, so a backing Let would buy nothing. The
Rem tables further down are the other case — a record or a list rebuilt on
Rem every call — and each of those is held by a Let now.

Def EngLinear() As Number
    0

Def EngLogModel() As Number
    1

Def EngExpModel() As Number
    2

Def EngPowerModel() As Number
    3

Rem ---- types -----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Record fields are positional: inserting one mid-record silently shifts
Rem every later constructor argument at every call site. If one of these
Rem grows, append.

Rem EngComplex is its own constructor rather than taking an EngComplexOf
Rem builder. A complex number is built ten times more often than any other
Rem record here, and EngComplexOf(3, 4) on every line would be intolerable.
Type EngComplex
    Re As Number
    Im As Number

Type EngPolar
    Mag   As Number
    Theta As Number

Rem Degs, not Deg: EngDeg is the radians converter and a machine word
Rem outranks a field accessor of the same name.
Type EngDms
    Degs As Number
    Mins As Number
    Secs As Number

Rem Avg/Sd/Total, not Mean/StdDev/Sum. All three of those are stats'
Rem words, this machine includes stats, and the word wins — the accessor
Rem would simply be unreachable. neural.shoddy met this first and named
Rem its scaler fields Center/Spread for the same reason.
Type EngStat
    Avg   As Number
    Sd    As Number
    Total As Number

Rem Named fields rather than A/B/R: a one-letter accessor is shadowed by
Rem any local or parameter of the same letter, and this machine is full of
Rem Let r = ... and Let b = ...
Type EngFit
    Intercept As Number
    Slope     As Number
    RFit      As Number

Type EngDivided
    Quotient  As List Of Number
    Remainder As List Of Number

Type EngUnit
    Name     As String
    Quantity As String
    Factor   As Number
    Offset   As Number

Type EngConst
    CName As String
    Value As Number
    Units As String

Rem ---- angles ----------------------------------------------------------

Def EngRad(deg As Number) As Number
    Rad(deg)

Def EngDeg(rad As Number) As Number
    Deg(rad)

Def EngGradOf(rad As Number) As Number
    rad * 200 / Pi()

Def EngFromGrad(grad As Number) As Number
    grad * Pi() / 200

Rem Degrees, minutes and seconds from a decimal degree, and back. The
Rem seconds carry the fraction, so a round trip is exact to the precision
Rem of the input rather than to the nearest second.
Def EngDmsOf(deg As Number) As EngDms
    Let sign = Ifte(deg < 0, 0 - 1, 1)
    Let a = Abs(deg)
    Let d = Fix(a)
    Let m = Fix((a - d) * 60)
    EngDms(sign * d, m, ((a - d) * 60 - m) * 60)

Def EngFromDms(d As EngDms) As Number
    Let sign = Ifte(Degs(d) < 0, 0 - 1, 1)
    sign * (Abs(Degs(d)) + Mins(d) / 60 + Secs(d) / 3600)

Rem ---- trigonometry the runtime does not supply -------------------------
Rem Sin, Cos, Tan, Asin, Acos, Atn and Atn2 are builtins; these are the
Rem reciprocals and the inverses that are not.

Def EngSec(x As Number) As Number
    1 / Cos(x)

Def EngCsc(x As Number) As Number
    1 / Sin(x)

Def EngCot(x As Number) As Number
    1 / Tan(x)

Def EngAsec(x As Number) As Number
    If Abs(x) < 1 Then
        Error("ENGASEC: NEEDS |X| >= 1")
    Else
        Acos(1 / x)

Def EngAcsc(x As Number) As Number
    If Abs(x) < 1 Then
        Error("ENGACSC: NEEDS |X| >= 1")
    Else
        Asin(1 / x)

Def EngAcot(x As Number) As Number
    Pi() / 2 - Atn(x)

Rem Vector direction and magnitude, from math.
Def EngAngleOf(dx As Number, dy As Number) As Number
    Angle(dx, dy)

Def EngHypot(x As Number, y As Number) As Number
    Hypot(x, y)

Def EngDistOf(x0 As Number, y0 As Number, x1 As Number, y1 As Number) As Number
    Dist(x0, y0, x1, y1)

Rem Polar and rectangular. Two words out rather than one returning a pair,
Rem because a caller wants one or the other at the point of use.
Def EngRectX(mag As Number, theta As Number) As Number
    mag * Cos(theta)

Def EngRectY(mag As Number, theta As Number) As Number
    mag * Sin(theta)

Def EngPolarOf(x As Number, y As Number) As EngPolar
    EngPolar(Hypot(x, y), Angle(x, y))

Rem ---- hyperbolics -----------------------------------------------------
Rem Tanh is a builtin; the rest are not. Sinh and Cosh overflow around
Rem |x| > 710, which is the ceiling stated in the banner.

Def EngSinh(x As Number) As Number
    (Exp(x) - Exp(0 - x)) / 2

Def EngCosh(x As Number) As Number
    (Exp(x) + Exp(0 - x)) / 2

Def EngTanh(x As Number) As Number
    Tanh(x)

Def EngSech(x As Number) As Number
    1 / EngCosh(x)

Def EngCsch(x As Number) As Number
    1 / EngSinh(x)

Def EngCoth(x As Number) As Number
    1 / Tanh(x)

Def EngAsinh(x As Number) As Number
    Log(x + Sqr(x * x + 1))

Def EngAcosh(x As Number) As Number
    If x < 1 Then
        Error("ENGACOSH: NEEDS X >= 1")
    Else
        Log(x + Sqr(x * x - 1))

Def EngAtanh(x As Number) As Number
    If Abs(x) >= 1 Then
        Error("ENGATANH: NEEDS |X| < 1")
    Else
        Log((1 + x) / (1 - x)) / 2

Def EngAsech(x As Number) As Number
    If x <= 0 Or x > 1 Then
        Error("ENGASECH: NEEDS 0 < X <= 1")
    Else
        EngAcosh(1 / x)

Def EngAcsch(x As Number) As Number
    EngAsinh(1 / x)

Def EngAcoth(x As Number) As Number
    If Abs(x) <= 1 Then
        Error("ENGACOTH: NEEDS |X| > 1")
    Else
        EngAtanh(1 / x)

Rem ---- logarithms, exponentials and roots ------------------------------

Def EngLogBase(base As Number, x As Number) As Number
    LogBase(base, x)

Def EngLog2(x As Number) As Number
    Log2(x)

Rem Accurate near zero, where Log(1 + x) and Exp(x) - 1 lose most of their
Rem significant digits to cancellation.
Def EngLog1p(x As Number) As Number
    Let u = 1 + x
    If u = 1 Then
        x
    Else
        Log(u) * x / (u - 1)

Def EngExpm1(x As Number) As Number
    Let u = Exp(x)
    If u = 1 Then
        x
    Else
        If Abs(x) > 0.5 Then
            u - 1
        Else
            (u - 1) * x / Log(u)

Rem The real nth root, including the odd-root-of-a-negative case that
Rem x ^ (1/n) gets wrong.
Def EngNthRoot(x As Number, n As Number) As Number
    If x < 0 Then
        If n Mod 2 = 0 Then
            Error("ENGNTHROOT: NO REAL EVEN ROOT OF A NEGATIVE")
        Else
            0 - ((0 - x) ^ (1 / n))
    Else
        x ^ (1 / n)

Rem ---- complex numbers -------------------------------------------------
Rem Nothing in the tree had a complex type before this. Every word that
Rem can return one does, rather than splitting into real and imaginary
Rem halves the caller has to reassemble.

Def EngCAdd(a As EngComplex, b As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(Re(a) + Re(b), Im(a) + Im(b))

Def EngCSub(a As EngComplex, b As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(Re(a) - Re(b), Im(a) - Im(b))

Def EngCMul(a As EngComplex, b As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(Re(a) * Re(b) - Im(a) * Im(b), Re(a) * Im(b) + Im(a) * Re(b))

Def EngCDiv(a As EngComplex, b As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    Let d = Re(b) * Re(b) + Im(b) * Im(b)
    If d = 0 Then
        Error("ENGCDIV: DIVISION BY ZERO")
    Else
        EngComplex((Re(a) * Re(b) + Im(a) * Im(b)) / d,
                   (Im(a) * Re(b) - Re(a) * Im(b)) / d)

Def EngConj(a As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(Re(a), 0 - Im(a))

Def EngCAbs(a As EngComplex) As Number
    Hypot(Re(a), Im(a))

Def EngCArg(a As EngComplex) As Number
    Angle(Re(a), Im(a))

Def EngCFromPolar(mag As Number, theta As Number) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(mag * Cos(theta), mag * Sin(theta))

Def EngCToPolar(a As EngComplex) As EngPolar
    EngPolar(EngCAbs(a), EngCArg(a))

Def EngCExp(a As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngCFromPolar(Exp(Re(a)), Im(a))

Def EngCLog(a As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngComplex(Log(EngCAbs(a)), EngCArg(a))

Def EngCSqrt(a As EngComplex) As EngComplex
    EngCFromPolar(Sqr(EngCAbs(a)), EngCArg(a) / 2)

Def EngCPow(a As EngComplex, n As Number) As EngComplex
    EngCFromPolar(EngCAbs(a) ^ n, EngCArg(a) * n)

Rem The only comparison offered. Structural equality on a record of
Rem doubles reports EngComplex(0.1 + 0.2, 0) = EngComplex(0.3, 0) as
Rem False, which is true of the bits and useless to the caller.
Def EngCNear(a As EngComplex, b As EngComplex, tol As Number) As Boolean
    EngCAbs(EngCSub(a, b)) <= tol

Rem Drops the zero part, and never prints a plus followed by a minus.
Def EngCShow(a As EngComplex) As String
    If Im(a) = 0 Then
        Str(Re(a))
    Else
        If Re(a) = 0 Then
            Str(Im(a)) & "i"
        Else
            If Im(a) < 0 Then
                Str(Re(a)) & " - " & Str(0 - Im(a)) & "i"
            Else
                Str(Re(a)) & " + " & Str(Im(a)) & "i"

Rem ---- statistics, wrapped ---------------------------------------------
Rem A face over stats.shoddy. Mandatory rather than polite: this machine
Rem includes stats, so these cannot be the bare names. The line is
Rem descriptive versus inferential - the hypothesis tests are not here,
Rem and a caller who wants Anova includes stats and says so.

Def EngSum(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Sum(xs)

Def EngMean(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Mean(xs)

Rem Both forms, each named for which it is: the wrong one gives a
Rem plausible wrong number and no error.
Def EngStdDev(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    StdDev(xs)

Def EngStdDevP(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    StdDevP(xs)

Def EngVar(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Var(xs)

Def EngVarP(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    VarP(xs)

Def EngMedian(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Median(xs)

Rem Quantile takes 0..1. Percentile (0..100) is deliberately not wrapped,
Rem so one fraction convention holds across the whole surface.
Def EngQuantile(xs As List Of Number, p As Number) As Number
    Quantile(xs, p)

Def EngMinimum(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Minimum(xs)

Def EngMaximum(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Maximum(xs)

Def EngRangeOf(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    RangeOf(xs)

Def EngSkewness(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Skewness(xs)

Def EngKurtosis(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Kurtosis(xs)

Def EngWeightedMean(xs As List Of Number, ws As List Of Number) As Number
    WeightedMean(xs, ws)

Def EngGeoMean(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    GeoMean(xs)

Def EngCorrel(xs As List Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    Correl(xs, ys)

Def EngCov(xs As List Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    Cov(xs, ys)

Def EngSpearman(xs As List Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    Spearman(xs, ys)

Def EngStat1(xs As List Of Number) As EngStat
    EngStat(Mean(xs), StdDev(xs), Sum(xs))

Rem Takes a raw mean and standard deviation where stats.NormCdf takes a
Rem z-score - the spec's shape, and one less conversion at the call site.
Def EngNormPdf(x As Number, mean As Number, sd As Number) As Number
    NormPdf(ZScore(x, mean, sd)) / sd

Def EngNormCdf(x As Number, mean As Number, sd As Number) As Number
    NormCdf(ZScore(x, mean, sd))

Def EngNormInv(p As Number) As Number
    NormInv(p)

Def EngTCdf(t As Number, df As Number) As Number
    TCdf(t, df)

Def EngTInv(p As Number, df As Number) As Number
    TInv(p, df)

Def EngChi2Cdf(x As Number, df As Number) As Number
    Chi2Cdf(x, df)

Def EngFCdf(f As Number, d1 As Number, d2 As Number) As Number
    FCdf(f, d1, d2)

Rem Transform, then one linear fit, repackaged so stats' Fit never leaves
Rem this machine. RFit is the correlation in the FITTED space, which is
Rem what every spreadsheet reports and is not the correlation of the raw
Rem data.
Def EngFitOf(xs As List Of Number, ys As List Of Number, model As Number) As EngFit
    If model = EngLinear() Then
        Let f = LinFit(xs, ys)
        EngFit(Intercept(f), Slope(f), Correl(xs, ys))
    Else
        If model = EngLogModel() Then
            If Minimum(xs) <= 0 Then
                Error("ENGFITOF: LOGARITHMIC MODEL NEEDS X > 0")
            Else
                Let lx = Map(xs, Log)
                EngFit(Intercept(LinFit(lx, ys)), Slope(LinFit(lx, ys)), Correl(lx, ys))
        Else
            If model = EngExpModel() Then
                If Minimum(ys) <= 0 Then
                    Error("ENGFITOF: EXPONENTIAL MODEL NEEDS Y > 0")
                Else
                    Let ly = Map(ys, Log)
                    EngFit(Exp(Intercept(LinFit(xs, ly))), Slope(LinFit(xs, ly)),
                           Correl(xs, ly))
            Else
                If model = EngPowerModel() Then
                    If Minimum(xs) <= 0 Or Minimum(ys) <= 0 Then
                        Error("ENGFITOF: POWER MODEL NEEDS X > 0 AND Y > 0")
                    Else
                        Let lx = Map(xs, Log)
                        Let ly = Map(ys, Log)
                        EngFit(Exp(Intercept(LinFit(lx, ly))), Slope(LinFit(lx, ly)),
                               Correl(lx, ly))
                Else
                    Error("ENGFITOF: UNKNOWN MODEL")

Rem ---- number theory ---------------------------------------------------
Rem Mod is infix in Shoddy: a Mod b, never Mod(a, b), which partially
Rem applies into a quotation and fails a long way from the mistake.

Def EngGcdGo(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    If b = 0 Then
        a
    Else
        EngGcdGo(b, a Mod b)

Def EngGcd(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    EngGcdGo(Abs(a), Abs(b))

Def EngLcm(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    If a = 0 Or b = 0 Then
        0
    Else
        Abs(a * b) / EngGcd(a, b)

Def EngIQuot(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    Fix(a / b)

Def EngIRem(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    a - b * Fix(a / b)

Rem Square-and-multiply, so no intermediate leaves 2^53 even when the
Rem exponent is large.
Def EngModPowGo(base As Number, e As Number, m As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If e = 0 Then
        acc
    Else
        If e Mod 2 = 1 Then
            EngModPowGo((base * base) Mod m, Fix(e / 2), m, (acc * base) Mod m)
        Else
            EngModPowGo((base * base) Mod m, Fix(e / 2), m, acc)

Def EngModPow(base As Number, e As Number, m As Number) As Number
    EngModPowGo(base Mod m, e, m, 1)

Rem Errors when there is no inverse: a caller handed a non-inverse back
Rem will not check it.
Def EngModInvGo(a As Number, m As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k >= m Then
        Error("ENGMODINV: NO INVERSE - A AND M ARE NOT COPRIME")
    Else
        If (a * k) Mod m = 1 Then
            k
        Else
            EngModInvGo(a, m, k + 1)

Def EngModInv(a As Number, m As Number) As Number
    If EngGcd(a, m) <> 1 Then
        Error("ENGMODINV: NO INVERSE - A AND M ARE NOT COPRIME")
    Else
        EngModInvGo(a Mod m, m, 1)

Def EngIsPrimeGo(n As Number, d As Number) As Boolean
    If d * d > n Then
        True
    Else
        If n Mod d = 0 Then
            False
        Else
            EngIsPrimeGo(n, d + 2)

Def EngIsPrime(n As Number) As Boolean
    If n < 2 Then
        False
    Else
        If n < 4 Then
            True
        Else
            If n Mod 2 = 0 Then
                False
            Else
                EngIsPrimeGo(n, 3)

Def EngPrimes(n As Number) As List Of Number
    Filter(Range(2, n), EngIsPrime)

Def EngFactorGo(n As Number, d As Number, acc As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If n = 1 Then
        acc
    Else
        If d * d > n Then
            Append(acc, n)
        Else
            If n Mod d = 0 Then
                EngFactorGo(n / d, d, Append(acc, d))
            Else
                EngFactorGo(n, d + 1, acc)

Def EngFactorize(n As Number) As List Of Number
    If n < 2 Then
        Error("ENGFACTORIZE: NEEDS N >= 2")
    Else
        EngFactorGo(n, 2, { })

Def EngDivisors(n As Number) As List Of Number
    Filter(Range(1, n), Fn(d) => n Mod d = 0)

Def EngTotient(n As Number) As Number
    CountIf(Range(1, n), Fn(k) => EngGcd(k, n) = 1)

Rem ---- combinatorics and the gamma family ------------------------------
Rem EngFact(18) is the last exact factorial: 19! is 1.2e17, past 2^53, and
Rem the result is quietly wrong rather than an error. EngComb multiplies
Rem and divides alternately for that reason - it reaches n = 1000 with no
Rem intermediate anywhere near a factorial.

Def EngFactGo(n As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If n <= 1 Then
        acc
    Else
        EngFactGo(n - 1, acc * n)

Def EngFact(n As Number) As Number
    If n < 0 Or n <> Fix(n) Then
        Error("ENGFACT: NEEDS A WHOLE NUMBER >= 0")
    Else
        EngFactGo(n, 1)

Def EngCombGo(n As Number, k As Number, i As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If i > k Then
        acc
    Else
        EngCombGo(n, k, i + 1, acc * (n - k + i) / i)

Def EngComb(n As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Or k > n Then
        0
    Else
        Round(EngCombGo(n, Min(k, n - k), 1, 1))

Def EngPermGo(n As Number, k As Number, i As Number, acc As Number) As Number
    If i >= k Then
        acc
    Else
        EngPermGo(n, k, i + 1, acc * (n - i))

Def EngPerm(n As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Or k > n Then
        0
    Else
        EngPermGo(n, k, 0, 1)

Def EngFibGo(n As Number, a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    If n <= 0 Then
        a
    Else
        EngFibGo(n - 1, b, a + b)

Def EngFib(n As Number) As Number
    If n < 0 Then
        Error("ENGFIB: NEEDS N >= 0")
    Else
        EngFibGo(n, 0, 1)

Rem Lanczos, g = 7, nine terms. The series is wrapped in one pair of
Rem brackets because a continuation line that begins with + or - outside
Rem brackets is a new statement, not more of this one.
Def EngLanczos(z As Number) As Number
    (0.99999999999980993
        + 676.5203681218851 / (z + 1)
        - 1259.1392167224028 / (z + 2)
        + 771.32342877765313 / (z + 3)
        - 176.61502916214059 / (z + 4)
        + 12.507343278686905 / (z + 5)
        - 0.13857109526572012 / (z + 6)
        + 0.0000099843695780195716 / (z + 7)
        + 0.00000015056327351493116 / (z + 8))

Rem The log of the gamma function, which is the one to reach for: it holds
Rem for arguments where EngGamma itself overflows, and every distribution
Rem below is built on it rather than on a ratio of factorials.
Def EngGammaLn(x As Number) As Number
    If x < 0.5 Then
        Log(Pi() / Abs(Sin(Pi() * x))) - EngGammaLn(1 - x)
    Else
        Let z = x - 1
        Let t = z + 7.5
        (0.5 * Log(2 * Pi()) + (z + 0.5) * Log(t) - t + Log(EngLanczos(z)))

Def EngGamma(x As Number) As Number
    If x < 0.5 Then
        Pi() / (Sin(Pi() * x) * EngGamma(1 - x))
    Else
        Exp(EngGammaLn(x))

Def EngBeta(a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    Exp(EngGammaLn(a) + EngGammaLn(b) - EngGammaLn(a + b))

Rem ---- discrete distributions ------------------------------------------
Rem Every Pdf takes the count first, because that is the quantity being
Rem asked about; every Cdf is the same word summed from zero and inclusive
Rem of k. stats.shoddy has the continuous side of this and is wrapped
Rem above; nothing here duplicates it.

Def EngBinomPdf(k As Number, n As Number, p As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Or k > n Then
        0
    Else
        EngComb(n, k) * (p ^ k) * ((1 - p) ^ (n - k))

Def EngBinomCdf(k As Number, n As Number, p As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Then
        0
    Else
        Sum(Map(Range(0, Min(k, n)), Fn(i) => EngBinomPdf(i, n, p)))

Def EngPoissonPdf(k As Number, lambda As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Then
        0
    Else
        Exp(0 - lambda + k * Log(lambda) - EngGammaLn(k + 1))

Def EngPoissonCdf(k As Number, lambda As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Then
        0
    Else
        Sum(Map(Range(0, k), Fn(i) => EngPoissonPdf(i, lambda)))

Rem k is the number of trials up to and including the first success, so k
Rem starts at 1. The other convention (failures before the first success)
Rem differs by one and gives an answer that looks reasonable either way.
Def EngGeomPdf(k As Number, p As Number) As Number
    If k < 1 Then
        0
    Else
        p * ((1 - p) ^ (k - 1))

Def EngGeomCdf(k As Number, p As Number) As Number
    If k < 1 Then
        0
    Else
        1 - ((1 - p) ^ k)

Rem Drawing without replacement: k successes in draws taken from a
Rem population of popn containing wins successes.
Def EngHyperPdf(k As Number, draws As Number, wins As Number, popn As Number) As Number
    If k < 0 Or k > draws Or k > wins Or draws - k > popn - wins Then
        0
    Else
        EngComb(wins, k) * EngComb(popn - wins, draws - k) / EngComb(popn, draws)

Def EngExpCdf(x As Number, rate As Number) As Number
    If x < 0 Then
        0
    Else
        1 - Exp(0 - rate * x)

Rem ---- numeric calculus ------------------------------------------------
Rem These take a quotation, [ Number -- Number ], and Call it. The step is
Rem scaled to the size of x: a fixed h is either noise near zero or lost in
Rem the rounding at 10^9.

Def EngStep(x As Number, scale As Number) As Number
    Max(Abs(x), 1) * scale

Def EngDeriv(f As [ Number -- Number ], x As Number) As Number
    Let h = EngStep(x, 0.0000001)
    (Call(f, x + h) - Call(f, x - h)) / (2 * h)

Rem A larger step than the first derivative wants. The second difference
Rem subtracts three numbers that are nearly equal, so too small an h loses
Rem every significant digit to cancellation.
Def EngDeriv2(f As [ Number -- Number ], x As Number) As Number
    Let h = EngStep(x, 0.0001)
    (Call(f, x + h) - 2 * Call(f, x) + Call(f, x - h)) / (h * h)

Rem Composite Simpson. n is the number of intervals and must be even; the
Rem alternating 4/2 weighting is what makes it Simpson rather than a
Rem trapezoid, and an odd n silently applies the wrong one.
Def EngIntegrate(f As [ Number -- Number ], a As Number, b As Number, n As Number) As Number
    If n < 2 Or n Mod 2 <> 0 Then
        Error("ENGINTEGRATE: N MUST BE EVEN AND >= 2")
    Else
        Let h = (b - a) / n
        Let s = Sum(Map(Range(1, n - 1),
                        Fn(i) => Ifte(i Mod 2 = 1, 4, 2) * Call(f, a + h * i)))
        h / 3 * (Call(f, a) + Call(f, b) + s)

Def EngSumOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    Sum(Map(Range(lo, hi), f))

Def EngProdOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    Product(Map(Range(lo, hi), f))

Rem ---- root-finding and optimisation -----------------------------------
Rem Fixed iteration counts rather than a tolerance loop, following
Rem stats.NormInvGo: a bounded number of halvings always terminates, where
Rem a tolerance test on doubles can chase a difference that never shrinks.

Def EngZeroGo(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k <= 0 Then
        (lo + hi) / 2
    Else
        Let mid = (lo + hi) / 2
        If Call(f, lo) * Call(f, mid) <= 0 Then
            EngZeroGo(f, lo, mid, k - 1)
        Else
            EngZeroGo(f, mid, hi, k - 1)

Rem Checks the bracket before descending. Bisection on a bracket with no
Rem sign change converges perfectly happily on an endpoint and returns a
Rem number that is not a root - the worst failure this machine could offer.
Def EngZeroOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    If Call(f, lo) * Call(f, hi) > 0 Then
        Error("ENGZEROOF: BRACKET DOES NOT CONTAIN A SIGN CHANGE")
    Else
        EngZeroGo(f, lo, hi, 100)

Rem Golden-section search. Requires the function to be unimodal on the
Rem bracket; given two minima it finds one of them and does not say which.
Def EngMinGo(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k <= 0 Then
        (lo + hi) / 2
    Else
        Let a = hi - (hi - lo) * 0.6180339887498949
        Let b = lo + (hi - lo) * 0.6180339887498949
        If Call(f, a) < Call(f, b) Then
            EngMinGo(f, lo, b, k - 1)
        Else
            EngMinGo(f, a, hi, k - 1)

Def EngMinOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    EngMinGo(f, lo, hi, 200)

Def EngMaxOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    EngMinGo(Fn(x) => 0 - Call(f, x), lo, hi, 200)

Rem Newton's method with a numeric derivative, so the caller supplies one
Rem quotation rather than two. Unbracketed and therefore unguarded: it
Rem diverges on a bad start and EngZeroOf is the safer word.
Def EngNewtonGo(f As [ Number -- Number ], x As Number, k As Number) As Number
    If k <= 0 Then
        x
    Else
        Let d = EngDeriv(f, x)
        If d = 0 Then
            Error("ENGNEWTONOF: DERIVATIVE WENT TO ZERO")
        Else
            EngNewtonGo(f, x - Call(f, x) / d, k - 1)

Def EngNewtonOf(f As [ Number -- Number ], x0 As Number) As Number
    EngNewtonGo(f, x0, 60)

Rem ---- polynomials -----------------------------------------------------
Rem COEFFICIENTS ARE HIGHEST POWER FIRST throughout. {1, 0, 0 - 4} is
Rem x^2 - 4. Reversed, every word here still returns a number, and it is
Rem the wrong number.

Def EngNthOr0(xs As List Of Number, i As Number) As Number
    Ifte(i < 1 Or i > Length(xs), 0, Nth(xs, Max(1, Min(i, Length(xs)))))

Def EngPolyEval(cs As List Of Number, x As Number) As Number
    Fold(cs, 0, Fn(a, c) => a * x + c)

Def EngPolyOr0(cs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If Length(cs) = 0 Then
        { 0 }
    Else
        cs

Def EngPolyTrim(cs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If Length(cs) <= 1 Then
        EngPolyOr0(cs)
    Else
        If Nth(cs, 1) = 0 Then
            EngPolyTrim(DropN(cs, 1))
        Else
            cs

Def EngPolyDegree(cs As List Of Number) As Number
    Length(EngPolyTrim(cs)) - 1

Def EngPolyAdd(a As List Of Number, b As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    Let n = Max(Length(a), Length(b))
    EngPolyTrim(Map(Range(1, n),
                    Fn(i) => EngNthOr0(a, i - (n - Length(a)))
                             + EngNthOr0(b, i - (n - Length(b)))))

Def EngPolyNeg(cs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    Map(cs, Fn(c) => 0 - c)

Def EngPolySub(a As List Of Number, b As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    EngPolyAdd(a, EngPolyNeg(b))

Def EngPolyScale(cs As List Of Number, k As Number) As List Of Number
    Map(cs, Fn(c) => c * k)

Def EngPolyMulTerm(a As List Of Number, b As List Of Number, k As Number) As Number
    Sum(Map(Range(1, Length(a)), Fn(i) => Nth(a, i) * EngNthOr0(b, k - i + 1)))

Def EngPolyMul(a As List Of Number, b As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If Length(a) = 0 Or Length(b) = 0 Then
        { 0 }
    Else
        EngPolyTrim(Map(Range(1, Length(a) + Length(b) - 1),
                        Fn(k) => EngPolyMulTerm(a, b, k)))

Rem Long division, one leading term at a time. Returns both halves in one
Rem record: a caller who wanted only the quotient has usually assumed the
Rem remainder is zero, and this makes them look.
Rem The accumulator is named left, not rem: Rem opens a comment, and a
Rem parameter of that name takes the rest of the line with it.
Def EngPolyDivGo(left As List Of Number, d As List Of Number,
                 quo As List Of Number) As EngDivided
    If Length(left) < Length(d) Then
        EngDivided(EngPolyOr0(quo), EngPolyOr0(left))
    Else
        Let f = Nth(left, 1) / Nth(d, 1)
        EngPolyDivGo(Map(Range(2, Length(left)),
                         Fn(i) => Nth(left, i) - f * EngNthOr0(d, i)),
                     d, Append(quo, f))

Def EngPolyDiv(a As List Of Number, b As List Of Number) As EngDivided
    Let d = EngPolyTrim(b)
    If Nth(d, 1) = 0 Then
        Error("ENGPOLYDIV: DIVISOR IS ZERO")
    Else
        EngPolyDivGo(EngPolyTrim(a), d, { })

Def EngPolyDeriv(cs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    Let n = Length(cs) - 1
    If n < 1 Then
        { 0 }
    Else
        Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Nth(cs, i) * (n - i + 1))

Rem c0 is the constant of integration, and is required rather than assumed
Rem to be zero: an indefinite integral without one is a particular
Rem antiderivative pretending to be the general answer.
Def EngPolyIntegral(cs As List Of Number, c0 As Number) As List Of Number
    Let n = Length(cs)
    Append(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(i) => Nth(cs, i) / (n - i + 1)), c0)

Def EngPolyArea(cs As List Of Number, a As Number, b As Number) As Number
    Let p = EngPolyIntegral(cs, 0)
    EngPolyEval(p, b) - EngPolyEval(p, a)

Rem Roots come back complex, always, even when they are real. A word that
Rem returned reals when it could and complex otherwise would have two
Rem return types and no caller could branch on it.
Def EngQuadRoots(a As Number, b As Number, c As Number) As List Of EngComplex
    If a = 0 Then
        Error("ENGQUADROOTS: NOT QUADRATIC - A IS ZERO")
    Else
        Let disc = b * b - 4 * a * c
        If disc >= 0 Then
            { EngComplex((0 - b + Sqr(disc)) / (2 * a), 0),
              EngComplex((0 - b - Sqr(disc)) / (2 * a), 0) }
        Else
            { EngComplex(0 - b / (2 * a), Sqr(0 - disc) / (2 * a)),
              EngComplex(0 - b / (2 * a), 0 - Sqr(0 - disc) / (2 * a)) }

Rem Cardano, by way of the trigonometric form for three real roots. The
Rem depressed cubic t^3 + pt + q is reached by substituting x = t - b/3a.
Def EngCubicReal(a As Number, b As Number, c As Number, d As Number) As List Of Number
    If a = 0 Then
        Error("ENGCUBICREAL: NOT CUBIC - A IS ZERO")
    Else
        Let shift = b / (3 * a)
        Let p = (3 * a * c - b * b) / (3 * a * a)
        Let q = (2 * b * b * b - 9 * a * b * c + 27 * a * a * d) / (27 * a * a * a)
        Let disc = q * q / 4 + p * p * p / 27
        If disc > 0 Then
            Let u = EngNthRoot(0 - q / 2 + Sqr(disc), 3)
            Let v = EngNthRoot(0 - q / 2 - Sqr(disc), 3)
            { u + v - shift }
        Else
            Let r = 2 * Sqr(0 - p / 3)
            Let phi = Acos(3 * q / (p * r)) / 3
            { r * Cos(phi) - shift,
              r * Cos(phi - 2 * Pi() / 3) - shift,
              r * Cos(phi - 4 * Pi() / 3) - shift }

Rem ---- numeric sequences -----------------------------------------------
Rem Sampling and differencing, the shapes a plot or a numeric experiment
Rem needs. seq.shoddy has the general list words and is included above;
Rem these are the ones that are arithmetic rather than structural.

Def EngLinSpace(lo As Number, hi As Number, n As Number) As List Of Number
    If n < 2 Then
        Error("ENGLINSPACE: NEEDS N >= 2")
    Else
        Map(Range(0, n - 1), Fn(i) => lo + (hi - lo) * i / (n - 1))

Rem Geometric spacing needs both ends strictly positive and on the same
Rem side of zero: there is no ratio that crosses it.
Def EngGeomSpace(lo As Number, hi As Number, n As Number) As List Of Number
    If n < 2 Then
        Error("ENGGEOMSPACE: NEEDS N >= 2")
    Else
        If lo <= 0 Or hi <= 0 Then
            Error("ENGGEOMSPACE: NEEDS BOTH ENDS > 0")
        Else
            Map(Range(0, n - 1), Fn(i) => lo * ((hi / lo) ^ (i / (n - 1))))

Def EngCumSum(xs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    Map(Range(1, Length(xs)), Fn(i) => Sum(Taken(xs, i)))

Rem One shorter than its input, which is the point: the k-th difference is
Rem between the k-th and the k+1-th, and there is no difference before the
Rem first element to pad with.
Def EngDiff(xs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If Length(xs) < 2 Then
        { }
    Else
        Map(Range(2, Length(xs)), Fn(i) => Nth(xs, i) - Nth(xs, i - 1))

Def EngMovAvg(xs As List Of Number, w As Number) As List Of Number
    If w < 1 Or w > Length(xs) Then
        Error("ENGMOVAVG: WINDOW MUST BE BETWEEN 1 AND THE LENGTH")
    Else
        Map(Range(1, Length(xs) - w + 1), Fn(i) => Mean(Taken(DropN(xs, i - 1), w)))

Rem A flat list scales to zeros rather than dividing by zero. Every value
Rem being the minimum is the honest reading of a constant input.
Def EngNormalize(xs As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    Let lo = Minimum(xs)
    Let hi = Maximum(xs)
    If hi = lo Then
        Map(xs, Fn(x) => 0)
    Else
        Map(xs, Fn(x) => (x - lo) / (hi - lo))

Def EngDotOf(xs As List Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    If Length(xs) <> Length(ys) Then
        Error("ENGDOTOF: LISTS ARE DIFFERENT LENGTHS")
    Else
        Sum(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(a, b) => a * b))

Def EngMagOf(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Sqr(EngDotOf(xs, xs))

Rem ---- units -----------------------------------------------------------
Rem A unit is a record, not a string, so a conversion between quantities
Rem is caught rather than performed. Factor and Offset take a value to the
Rem quantity's base unit: base = x * Factor + Offset. Offset is zero for
Rem everything except temperature, which is why it exists at all.

Def EngToBase(x As Number, u As EngUnit) As Number
    x * Factor(u) + Offset(u)

Def EngFromBase(x As Number, u As EngUnit) As Number
    (x - Offset(u)) / Factor(u)

Def EngConvert(x As Number, src As EngUnit, dst As EngUnit) As Number
    If Quantity(src) <> Quantity(dst) Then
        Error("ENGCONVERT: " & Quantity(src) & " AND " & Quantity(dst)
              & " ARE DIFFERENT QUANTITIES")
    Else
        EngFromBase(EngToBase(x, src), dst)

Rem Length, base metre.
Let unitMetre = EngUnit("METRE", "LENGTH", 1, 0)

Def EngMetre() As EngUnit
    unitMetre

Let unitKilometre = EngUnit("KILOMETRE", "LENGTH", 1000, 0)

Def EngKilometre() As EngUnit
    unitKilometre

Let unitCentimetre = EngUnit("CENTIMETRE", "LENGTH", 0.01, 0)

Def EngCentimetre() As EngUnit
    unitCentimetre

Let unitMillimetre = EngUnit("MILLIMETRE", "LENGTH", 0.001, 0)

Def EngMillimetre() As EngUnit
    unitMillimetre

Let unitInch = EngUnit("INCH", "LENGTH", 0.0254, 0)

Def EngInch() As EngUnit
    unitInch

Let unitFoot = EngUnit("FOOT", "LENGTH", 0.3048, 0)

Def EngFoot() As EngUnit
    unitFoot

Let unitYard = EngUnit("YARD", "LENGTH", 0.9144, 0)

Def EngYard() As EngUnit
    unitYard

Let unitMile = EngUnit("MILE", "LENGTH", 1609.344, 0)

Def EngMile() As EngUnit
    unitMile

Let unitNauticalMile = EngUnit("NAUTICAL MILE", "LENGTH", 1852, 0)

Def EngNauticalMile() As EngUnit
